 it  rather than like dictating  no this is how you say this line this is  you know when you actually you it's a  collaboration between you and the actor  where you're trying to take you're  discussing it like that  emotion there the way you did that lot  you know and then you you can shape the  whole piece like that in the moment  and i think jody and shayleen who are  very different kind of actors you know  they're  very much in the more in the hollywood  mold you know  they they come very prepared they've  thought about their intention in the  scene in that moment in that line and  they  do it like that they were really stunned  by him i think  and loved you know they said to me after  the first day god that's like  you know having your own private one-man  theater show he's like doing all these  different things in front of you and  they find it incredibly entertaining  to watch him and um  so so and he's also alan cookler who's  the  who's the dp on this was an old friend  of mine and  he and i just loved shooting dahar  because he's very light aware he knows  where  there's a really interesting light he'd  always find an interesting shadow or the  interesting highlight or whatever  i think subconsciously i don't think  he's thinking about that stuff but he's  very just  he thinks like a filmmaker rather than  like a so he's like a theater act in one  way but he's a very film friendly actor  in another way so you know the first  scene we shot with him because jody got  really ill  over the christmas break that we had and  she couldn't come back  and and so we had to bring a lot of  stuff forward to do other things to you  know rearrange the schedule  and the very first day that shahar had  was his end  speech that he has to give at the end of  the film he's been through everything  and i was like oh good oh disaster  and dahar was like playing it very cool  playing very cool but i knew he must be  this like  cursing me in the situation and he  turned up  and he said looking into the lens at the  end of the camera  speaking right into the camera and he  delivers this speech the first time  so powerfully so movingly and alvin and  i look at each other and like  oh my god he is going to be brilliant  and as we knew right from that very  first  take that he totally had you know he  immersed himself into this person  so deeply and uh yeah from there on it  you know it got better and better and he  he he he  the funny thing about is i've worked  with actors who are you know very  immersive before  and kind of you know methody and that  could be really  a nightmare for a director as well  because they never switch off  and um you know they're totally  humorless about everything  but tahar's like you know then he's like  out chatting with everybody hugging the  makeup people  having one of his three packets of  cigarettes a day his 10th espresso  being charming french i mean it's  amazing amazing  that uh that's fantastic um  uh i have so many questions and i'm  running out of time so i'm trying to  figure out what i want to actually bring  up with you  um i guess i want to talk a little bit  about the editing because  this is something as you mentioned that  you shot certain scenes a number of  different ways talk a little bit about  the challenge of  crafting everything in the editing room  and also i'm always obsessed with like  if a director had a much longer cut  uh before uh finalizing yeah a longer  car  my editor got back from shooting in  mauritanian south africa to my editor  justine who i've worked with for  23 years now um and  she was like pale and i said what's  wrong with you  well the you know the the assembly is  three hours and 15 minutes  i don't know how we're gonna get this  down and  uh you know i was like well it's better  to be too long or too short i suppose  and uh we we you know we had we got it  down to two and a half hours it was a  really nice cut at two and a half hours  but it's obviously still too long and  from there it was painful taking things  out  and so you know the movie is still just  over two hours  um and uh you know that was already  already  already already long so yeah there is a  longer there's a long cup i mean i think  what  what what exists what the challenge with  the editing  was that this is you know it's an  incredibly complex structure by far the  most complex structure i've ever  tried in a film because you've got three  different time periods  and you've got three different character  main characters who you're following  each individually at different times  so you know that's 3d chess right there  you know it's really  really complex and so a lot of our time  was spent you know obviously  nuancing individual scenes but then a  lot of times spent  moving these bits around a little bit  you know can you put that  there or we need we feel like need a  little more of jody or we need it you  know  this that and that's also why a lot of  decisions were made when we were  shooting it about different styles of  shooting different use of color  and different aspect ratios in order to  have a psychological effect but also to  differentiate  between different periods of the  audience you know hopefully not noticing  those things consciously but they're  they're not lost they know i'm here i'm  there i mean in that time  and and i think that's one of the things  i i think justine did an incredible job  because  i haven't heard anybody you know many  criticisms but i've never heard anyone  say i was confused which is amazing can  you consider all of that  all of that material believe me i  i could talk a whole bunch about the  aspect ratio changes  but i i am curious because this this  story has  so much in it um normally uh i'm like  okay i don't need to see  the deleted scenes or whatever but is  the cut that you had at two and a half  hours something that's like finished  where at some point you might release an  extended cut or  something you know how it goes these  days i mean it's sort of like you know  if this movie makes any money which is  hard when the  theaters are closed um then they might  spend a little more money on  on that back end and i would love to do  that because i think it is i think it is  interesting and because it's not a movie  with  a ton of special effects uh because it's  mostly in camera  um it's not that expensive to do a you  know a  a cheap kind of video finish on  something so yeah we  we we i would love i would love to do  that  and i think um you know because it is is  because so many characters are in this  um i think in some ways it can benefit  from  it can benefit from that because you get  a little more time with everybody  there's so much to this story  and i i do think that you guys did an  amazing job with the edit keeping  everything going  because it's over different periods and  i think the aspect ratios really helped  and  the different you know color and  filmmaking yeah  yeah no i i i agree um on that note i'm  gonna stop and just say  congrats and uh we did not talk about  iguanas but next time  oh yeah i love the iguana scene one of  my favorite scenes  okay don't that really nice really nice  to chat and  thanks for asking interesting questions  for the end of my day
 

